@org
; Movement-script entry-point table (41 entries, half-byte-offset into $5BAF)
;
; .
;
; .
;
; Read by #R$BB62's object-init tail using a byte read from the fixed-slot
; address (template bytes 7/8, see #R$5B55) as index - not from the template
; directly. The resulting byte is doubled and added to $5BAF to give a byte
; offset into the shared movement-script bytecode pool there, stored at
; IX+$15/$16 as the script start address (the field #R$B94E reads scripts
; from). Values are strictly ascending ($00-$89, i.e. real offsets 0-274) -
; stored halved so a single byte can address the whole pool. Scripts are
; variable-length (no fixed record size), so consecutive entries aren't evenly
; spaced. Since the index comes from a live fixed-slot byte rather than a
; template constant, the chosen script can vary at runtime.
b$5B00 DEFB $00,$02,$04,$09,$10,$17,$1E,$26
 $5B08 DEFB $2A,$31,$38,$3C,$44,$48,$4D,$4F
 $5B10 DEFB $52,$55,$58,$5A,$5F,$62,$65,$67
 $5B18 DEFB $6A,$6D,$72,$75,$7C,$7F,$84,$89
 $5B20 DEFB $8E,$91,$95,$97,$9C,$9D,$9E,$9F
 $5B28 DEFB $A0

; Formation-tag to group-index lookup table (44 entries, indexed by tag & $3F)
;
; .
;
; .
;
; Indexed by #R$BA5F from the spawn-timeline's tag byte (C, masked to bits 0-5,
; though only 44 entries are populated); each entry is a group index used to
; look up the per-group spawn budget in the table at $5CF4. Values are strictly
; ascending ($00-$FB), consistent with a byte-offset/threshold table rather
; than raw group indices.
b$5B29 DEFB $00,$04,$09,$0D,$11,$15,$19,$1E
 $5B31 DEFB $26,$30,$38,$40,$48,$50,$58,$5D
 $5B39 DEFB $61,$66,$6B,$6F,$73,$77,$7E,$85
 $5B41 DEFB $89,$90,$95,$9C,$A4,$A8,$B3,$BA
 $5B49 DEFB $C1,$C5,$CA,$D1,$D8,$DD,$E1,$E5
 $5B51 DEFB $E9,$F0,$F4,$FB

; Spawn-template table (9 bytes/entry, indexed by #R$BA5F as index*9 + $5B55)
;
; .
;
; .
;
; Each 9-byte template initializes a newly-spawned object (field mapping
; confirmed by matching self-modified write targets to their instruction
; operands in #R$BB62's init block): byte 0 -> IX+$05; byte 1 -> IX+$12; byte 2
; -> IX+$1A; byte 3 (+difficulty offset $9C41, clamped) -> IX+$1B; byte 4 ->
; IX+$11; bytes 5/6 (little-endian) -> IX+$02/$03; bytes 7/8 (little-endian) ->
; a fixed-slot address ($9D1E/$9D24/$9D2A, one of #R$B730's 4 persistent object
; slots - $9D30 never observed). IX+$02/$03 is NOT a code pointer despite
; resembling one: every one of the 10 templates' values resolves exactly to
; either a record on the #R$6B42 17-byte descriptor grid ($6B42+17*n -
; $6BCA=record 8, $6B64/$6B75/$6B86/$6BA8=records 2/3/4/6) or an entry in the
; #R$6BDB 6-byte explosion/state-descriptor table ($6BDB+6*n -
; $6BDB/$6BF9/$6BFF/$6C05=entries 0/5/6/7); $6B42's own IY+$02/$03 field is
; itself something #R$7DC9 *writes* (a decoded graphics sub-pointer) rather
; than executable code, and the raw bytes at these addresses don't decode as
; coherent Z80. So IX+$02/$03 is an index/pointer into one of two shared
; descriptor-record tables, read as data by an as-yet-unlocated per-object
; routine, not called as a handler. Bytes 7/8's address is used twice: #R$BB62
; reads one byte from it to select the object's movement script (see #R$5B00),
; and after all objects for this call are spawned, a word at that address is
; read, swapped via EX (SP),HL, and written back - apparently linking the slot
; to the newly-spawned object(s), exact mechanism not fully traced. Byte 1 also
; happens to land on $5B56, the same address written directly as a
; screen/timing poke by #R$A612's config word $0A and timeline command $FB -
; unrelated dual use of that literal address, not a field alias.
b$5B55 DEFB $0B,$06,$00,$20,$00
 $5B5A DEFW $6BDB,$9D24
 $5B5E DEFB $09,$01,$00,$10,$01
 $5B63 DEFW $6B64,$9D1E
 $5B67 DEFB $05,$01,$81,$08,$04
 $5B6C DEFW $6B86,$9D2A
 $5B70 DEFB $0C,$07,$83,$18,$01
 $5B75 DEFW $6BA8,$9D2A
 $5B79 DEFB $0E,$1E,$81,$C0,$02
 $5B7E DEFW $6C05,$9D1E
 $5B82 DEFB $06,$01,$80,$00,$04
 $5B87 DEFW $6B86,$9D2A
 $5B8B DEFB $0A,$01,$00,$14,$01
 $5B90 DEFW $6B75,$9D1E
 $5B94 DEFB $11,$01,$80,$10,$01
 $5B99 DEFW $6BF9,$9D1E
 $5B9D DEFB $11,$01,$80,$10,$01
 $5BA2 DEFW $6BFF,$9D1E
 $5BA6 DEFB $0C,$1A,$81,$48,$01
 $5BAB DEFW $6BA8,$9D2A

; Shared movement-script bytecode pool (variable-length scripts, entry points
; via $5B00)
;
; .
;
; .
;
; One continuous stream of movement-script bytecode in #R$B8F9/#R$B920's format
; (bit 7 set = wait byte; else bits 0-2 select one of 8 opcodes via
; $A076/$A082, bits 3-6 are the opcode's parameter). #R$BB62 computes an entry
; point as $5BAF + 2*(byte from $5B00) and stores it at a spawned object's
; IX+$15/$16; #R$B94E then reads bytes sequentially from there. Not an array of
; fixed-size records - each script runs until its own content ends.
b$5BAF DEFB $22,$80,$18,$20
 $5BB3 DEFB $62,$80,$10,$20
 $5BB7 DEFB $0D,$11,$02,$0F,$30,$22,$80,$10
 $5BBF DEFB $20,$00
